(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).afoqt=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt.full||[]).push({
  num: 4, part: `Part Two: The Truth`, title: `Integrity`, weeks: `March 6 to 7, 2027`,
  tasks: [`SJ`,`AR`,`VA`,`AI`,`TR`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Two Tuesdays went by without the sentence, and Vern asked once.`,
    `On the first one he asked as she sat down, and she said not yet. He nodded and opened the reading book where they'd left off. On the second he let it be. At the end of the night he handed her a log out of the box, the way he always did, and she put it in her coat without reading it. LETTER 2 sat on the laptop next to LETTER, and she left them both shut.`,
    `Saturday, March 6, was drill. At ten to four in the morning the launch board in the shop had two two eight flying at eight thirty, and next to it, in Boyd's block printing, DARROW. The full timers had left a note on the board from Friday's postflight. One of the tires on the left main gear was worn to limits. It had to come off before the jet went anywhere.`,
    `Fisk came in at five after four with his ears red and the scanner lanyard still around his neck. Nine and a half hours at the distribution center, he said, when she asked. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Quick, upbeat, worn thin.">"I'm good, Sergeant."</span> She looked at him a second longer than she needed to, and then she let it go. He'd done four wheel changes with her since the fall, and one of Kemp's airmen was free to help. She gave him the wheel and went to start her walkaround.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Vern's Nav Table: integrity, and saying it straight`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The exception, up close`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"I held a jet on the ground over my own arithmetic more than once. Nobody on the ramp ever thanked me for it. Nobody ever had to."</span>`,
          `You've had the rule twice now, and its one exception. Here's how to tell when the exception applies. Ask three things. Is something false or unsafe? Is it about to be used, by a jet, a report, a commander, anyone who'll sign or act on it? And is it still moving, not already caught and fixed? If all three are yes, the most effective action usually stops it and tells the person who needs to know, right away.`,
          `That doesn't throw out the rest of the key. You still go to your own supervisor before the office above them. You still say it face to face, not on a channel the whole base hears. You can still give the person involved a private word first, as long as it doesn't hold up the stop or the report. What changes is the clock.`,
          `Notice what the most effective option in these items usually leaves out. It doesn't decide what happens to the person. That belongs to the chain above you. Your part is to stop it, report it, and tell the truth about your own share of it.`],
        terms: [[`Integrity breach`,`A false record, a false signature, or a safety problem somebody's hiding.`],[`In motion`,`About to be used: by a jet, a report, or someone who'll sign or act on it.`]],
        exam: `When an option quietly fixes the problem and tells nobody, look at it hard for least effective. A quiet fix leaves the false record in place, and the habit with it.` },
      { h: `Integrity items`,
        body: [`Integrity and Professionalism items tend to share a shape. Somebody did something wrong, maybe you, and one of the options makes it go away without anyone finding out. Signing for work you didn't see. Rounding a number up for a boss. Covering for a friend. Keeping a gift you shouldn't. That option is rarely the most effective, and it's often the least.`,
          `Your own mistakes count too. When the error is yours, the effective move is to report it yourself, early, to the person it affects, along with what you've already done about it. Waiting to see whether anybody notices is the low one.`,
          `A signature says you did the thing or saw it done. On the flight line and on this test, signing for work you didn't do or didn't see is a false record, even if the work turns out fine.`,
          `Loyalty doesn't change this. The key does reward taking care of your people. It doesn't reward protecting them from the record of what they did. Taking care of them is what happens after the record's straight.`],
        terms: [[`Covering`,`Hiding or quietly fixing someone's mistake so the people above you don't learn of it.`]],
        exam: `If the problem is yours, look for the option where you report it before anybody asks.` },
      { h: `Communication items`,
        body: [`Communication items are about who hears what, from whom, and when. Bad news goes early, and it comes from you. If you promised your people something and it fell through, tell them yourself, say why, and say what happens next. Letting them read it off a schedule is lower. Blaming the people above you is lower still.`,
          `When you're carrying out a decision you didn't make and don't like, explain it to your people the way it was explained to you, and take your disagreement up the chain in private. Running the decision down in front of your airmen is one of the lowest picks there is. It costs your commander their trust in you, and it costs you your airmen's trust in both of you.`,
          `If something changes another person's work, they should hear it from you directly. The secondhand version is the one with the qualifiers gone.`,
          `Communication runs up the chain too. If you made a call your supervisor is going to hear about, tell them first, yourself, with what you did and why. A boss who hears it from somebody else has to wonder what else they haven't heard.`],
        terms: [[`Up the chain`,`To your own supervisor first, then theirs, in order.`]],
        exam: `When you disagree with an order, look for the option that carries it out and questions it in private.` },
      { h: `Right words, wrong room`,
        body: [`The hardest pairs on this subtest are two options that say the same thing. One says it to the person, privately. The other says it in a huddle, on a radio, or in an email to the whole section. The content's right in both, and the audience decides it.`,
          `Timing works the same way. Telling your supervisor on Monday is fine in a normal week and wrong if the jet flies Saturday. Before you pick most effective, ask who hears it and when. Before you pick least, look for the option that does real harm: a false record kept, a person shamed in front of others, a supervisor skipped.`],
        terms: [[`Partial`,`An option that's right in what it does and wrong in where, when, or to whom.`]],
        exam: `Two options with the same message? Prefer the one that reaches the right people soonest without an audience it doesn't need.` },
      { h: `Warmups in test order`,
        body: [`On test day the subtests come in one order, back to back. Verbal Analogies runs eight minutes and hands you straight to twenty nine minutes of word problems. Practice them that way at least once a week: a timed analogy block, then arithmetic, with no break between. The switch costs you a minute the first few times, and you'd rather spend that minute at a kitchen table than in a test center.`,
          `Before a practice sitting, write the order and the minutes down from the current Pearson page, and check what you wrote against the page. A practice run on the wrong clock teaches the wrong pace.`],
        terms: [[`Test order`,`The sequence the subtests run in on test day. Check it against the current Pearson page.`]],
        exam: `Read the first word problem after the analogies twice. Right after a fast subtest is an easy place for a careless miss.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f4s1`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`Slack`, q:`How much slack is in her morning, at the shop's numbers?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Fisk and the airman had the jack under the gear and the first tool out at 4:40. Kit wrote the time on the back of a work card, the way she wrote down everything on a launch morning.`,`The shop's planning number for a main wheel was an hour and a half with two people. After that came her inspection, twenty minutes if she took her time, and she always took it. The crew bus was due at seven. She wanted the jet done, wheel and inspection both, before the bus doors opened.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fifty minutes, from the wheel being done to the bus.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She gets fifty and has it half written before she sees what's missing. Her own twenty minutes. She'd left herself out of it.`,
          why:`You counted the job and forgot the check that comes after it. On a word problem the step people drop is usually the one they do themselves. Write every piece down before you add.`},
        {t:`An hour and ten, seven less four forty less the work.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She takes 4:40 from 7:00 the way you'd take forty cents from seven dollars and gets two sixty, which isn't a time. An hour doesn't have a hundred minutes. She does it over in minutes.`,
          why:`Clock time isn't decimal. Seven o'clock less four forty is two hours and twenty minutes, not two sixty. When a problem mixes hours and minutes, put it all in minutes first.`},
        {t:`Thirty minutes, after the wheel and her inspection.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Ninety minutes from 4:40 is 6:10. Her twenty makes it 6:30. Thirty minutes to the bus. She wrote 30 at the bottom of the card and circled it, and stopped thinking about it until it was gone.`,
          why:`Start, plus the job, plus the check, against the deadline. Ninety minutes is an hour thirty, so 6:10, then 6:30, then thirty minutes to seven. The wrong choices on this subtest are built out of the steps people skip. Did you count every one?`},
        {t:`Ten minutes, with the wheel at an hour fifty.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She reads an hour and a half as an hour fifty and gets ten minutes of slack, and a knot in her stomach she doesn't need. It's an hour thirty.`,
          why:`An hour and a half is ninety minutes. Half an hour is thirty minutes, not fifty. Turn halves and quarters into minutes before you do anything else with them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s2`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`AI`, title:`Into the wind`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 5:30 she came around the nose with her flashlight and found Fisk on his knees by the old tire, the bead finally broken loose. He was working the bar one slow pull at a time. Twice she watched him stop with his eyes shut and start again, and the second time she opened her mouth and then went on with her walkaround. The wind had swung around in the night. Out at midfield the windsock stood straight out the other way from Friday, and the tower had turned the runway around to match it.`,`Fisk sat back on his heels and looked out at the sock for a while before he said anything. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"They're going out the long way. Why not let the wind push them?"</span> He asked it the way you'd ask somebody the time.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him a headwind gets the wings to flying speed in less runway.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Wind in her face is free airspeed."</span> She held her glove up into it, flat, and the river wind about took it off her hand. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Fifteen knots of that and she's got fifteen knots over the wings parked at the hold line. Turn her around and she has to buy those fifteen back with runway, and fifteen more on top."</span> Fisk held his own glove up and squinted at it like it might tell him something. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"So the tailwind's a loan."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"At a lousy rate. On a jet this heavy you want every foot."</span>`,
          why:`A wing doesn't read the ground. I flew with an aircraft commander out of Tolliver who called the windsock the cheapest engine on the field, and he'd turn a runway around for five knots. Point the nose into the wind and part of your airspeed is already there before the brakes come off, so you lift off with runway to spare. Wind at your back, you pay for it in concrete.`},
        {t:`Tell him a tailwind adds to the speed, and either way works.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fisk nods. For about a second it sounds right to both of them. Then she looks at the tower, lit up like a lunchbox out there at five in the morning, and figures nobody turns a whole runway around for the fun of it.`,
          why:`Ground speed, sure. Wing doesn't read ground speed. I watched a lieutenant take a tailwind he called light out of a field in Kansas once, and he used up most of the concrete getting off. Wind at your back, you pay in runway.`},
        {t:`Tell him it's for stopping, if they have to abort the takeoff.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Huh. Okay."</span> She isn't wrong, and she isn't right either. A headwind does help if the pilots end up standing on the brakes halfway down the runway, she'll give him that. Boyd would have called it answering the question on the back of the page.`,
          why:`Half credit. Into the wind, sure, you stop shorter on an abort. That's not why the tower flipped it, though. They flipped it for the takeoff roll, the end with the free airspeed.`},
        {t:`Tell him the tower picks the end that's quieter for the town.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"At five in the morning?"</span> He has her. Nobody in Tolliver is awake to complain but the bakery and the guy who runs the grain elevator.`,
          why:`Some fields do pick an end for the neighbors. At five in the morning with the sock pointing the other way, nobody's flipping a runway for the neighbors. Wind first, then everything else.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s3`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The hook`, q:`The torque line is signed, and the wrench never left its hook. What should Kit do first?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 6:10 Fisk came up on the radio. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Quick, casual, a little too bright.">"Wheel's on, Sergeant. Forms are done."</span> Kemp had pulled his airman off at six for his own jet, so Fisk had finished alone. Kit was across the jet with her head in the oil panel, and she said she'd be over.`,`She read the forms on the hood of her truck first. Wheel removed, wheel installed, and the torque line signed done at 6:05, with his initials next to it. Then she walked to the tool crib for the big torque wrench. Boyd had raised her to check the torque on anything with wheels, everybody's, her own included, and she'd never found a reason to stop.`,`The big wrench was on its hook behind the crib window, in its case, with the calibration tag still wired to the handle. The airman on the window turned the log around for her without being asked. The last line for that wrench was Thursday afternoon.`,`It was the only wrench in the crib that went that high. Nobody had torqued the wheel. A nut run down hard by hand looks a lot like a torqued one, and there was no torque stripe on it either, which you'd only catch if you went looking. That was a wheel carrying its share of three hundred thousand pounds down a runway on somebody's guess. Out past the crib window the crew bus was turning in at the far gate.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask around the ramp, in case anybody had another big wrench out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She spends ten minutes asking. Nobody had one. There's only one. By the time she keys the radio to hold the jet, the bus is parked at the nose and the aircraft commander is watching her from its steps.`,
          why:`You already knew. Asking around was hoping the record would turn out true. When something false is headed for a jet, every minute you spend hoping, the jet gets closer to flying on it. Stop it first, then find out the rest.`},
        {t:`Call the expediter and hold two two eight until the wheel's torqued.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She keyed the radio. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Crisp, firm, controlled.">"Expediter, two two eight. Hold my jet. Left main needs torque."</span> A pause on the net. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Copy, holding. How long?"</span> She said she'd call him. She wrote the wheel up in the forms with a red X, so the jet was grounded on paper as well as on the net. Then she signed the big wrench out under her own name, and the kid on the window wrote the time down without looking up at her.`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism, and this is the exception. A false record was headed for a jet. The rule's still talk to the person first. The jet just doesn't sit around waiting on that talk. You stopped it at your own level with one call, and you said what was wrong without saying who. The who comes next. Which would you rather explain to a crew, a late jet or a wheel?`},
        {t:`Sign out the wrench and torque the wheel herself before the bus comes.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She signs the wrench out and runs. She's on her knees at the wheel when the bus pulls up, and the aircraft commander wants to know why his crew chief is torquing a wheel the forms say is done. She has to hold the jet anyway, late, in front of him.`,
          why:`Right instinct, wrong order. You'd have fixed the wheel and left the false line sitting in the forms, and you'd have raced a crew bus to do it. When a record's wrong, stop the jet first. Then do the work at the speed the work takes.`},
        {t:`Raise Fisk on the radio and ask him whether he torqued it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Fisk, did you torque that wheel?"</span> The whole ramp is on that frequency. There's a long gap, and then his voice, small. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Small, halting, caught.">"Stand by, Sergeant."</span> Every crew chief on the line hears him not answer.`,
          why:`You asked the right question on the worst channel there is. Every radio on the ramp heard it, and he had to answer in front of all of them. Hold the jet on the radio. Ask the person face to face.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s4`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Bring the light`, q:`Fisk is in the truck with two of Kemp's airmen. How should Kit start?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=s.picks||{};return [`The crew truck was idling by the fence. ${p[2]===3?`Fisk was in the passenger seat, awake now, staring out the windshield with the radio in his lap.`:`Fisk had his head on the passenger window, out cold sitting up, lanyard and all.`} In the back, Kemp's two airmen had their hoods up. Neither one was asleep.`,`Then Boyd's voice on the radio. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Darrow. Expediter says your jet's on hold."</span> She told him she'd come to him in fifteen minutes. He said copy and nothing else, which from Boyd meant he'd be standing at his truck in fourteen.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Open the truck door and ask him there why his initials are on it.`, s:1, flag:`publiccorrection`, d:{trust:-2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She opens the door. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Fisk. Why are your initials on a torque that never happened?"</span> He wakes up into it. In the back seat two hoods come down. He tells her the truth, all of it, and he tells it to three people. By noon the whole flight has it, with a few things added.`,
          why:`Every word true, and two airmen got to watch him learn he'd been caught. It's the right talk in the wrong room, and he'll remember the room longer than the talk. You could have walked him thirty yards. Why didn't you?`},
        {t:`Torque it herself, let him sleep for now, and talk to him later.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She leaves him asleep and does the wheel herself, and it's right when she's done. Her plan is to let him sleep through the launch and have the talk at next drill, four weeks off. Then she slams the wrench case shut, and across the ramp the crew truck door opens. His initials are still in the forms, on a line that wasn't true at 6:05.`,
          why:`You did his work and meant to put his conversation off a month. The wheel got fixed. Fisk didn't. Taking the job over feels kind at six in the morning, and it leaves the person and the record right where they were.`},
        {t:`Wait for Boyd to get there, and let Boyd do the asking.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`She stands by the truck with the forms and waits for Boyd. Fisk wakes up on his own, sees her face through the glass, and gets out and walks over to the wheel without being told. Neither of them says a word. Then Boyd on the radio, flat. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"My truck, Darrow."</span> He'll be starting the hard part cold, and Fisk keeps looking at her, not at the radio.`,
          why:`He's your airman. Handing the hard talk to the flight chief skips the level that's yours. Boyd needs to know, and soon. He shouldn't be the first person Fisk hears it from.`},
        {t:`Get him out of the truck and walk him to the far side of the jet.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, clear, very level.">"Fisk. Bring my light."</span> He got out fast. She walked him around the nose to the far side, under the left wing by the new wheel, where nobody in the truck could see them. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, clear, very level.">"The big wrench never left the crib."</span> He didn't try to talk his way out of it, and later that was the part she kept. He just nodded at the wheel, once. Then they torqued it together, Fisk reading her each step out of the tech data while she leaned on the handle, and after that she did her inspection with him standing there. Nobody signed a thing on that wheel until it was true.`,
          why:`Integrity, with Mentoring in it. The jet was already held, so the hurry had gone out of the morning. That bought him a few minutes away from the truck to tell it himself. He still had to tell Boyd. He just didn't have to tell an audience first. Once the jet's stopped, who should hear it first?`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s5`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Boyd's truck`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const pc=(s.flags||{}).publiccorrection||(G||{}).publiccorrection;return [`Boyd's truck was at the end of the row with the engine running and the window down. ${pc?`Kemp's two airmen were already out of the crew truck and walking to their own jet, slowly, looking back.`:`Nobody else on the ramp was paying any attention to two two eight yet.`}`,`Fisk was by the new wheel on the cold side of the jet with his hands in his pockets, and he got to it before she could. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Quiet, ashamed, getting it out.">"I said the forms were done. On the radio, at ten after. I knew the big wrench was still on its hook when I keyed the mike."</span>`,`He'd signed the line so he wouldn't forget it, he said, and meant to go get the wrench, and sat down in the truck to warm his hands first. She asked him why he hadn't just told her at four that he was done in. He kicked the chock, not hard. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Low, miserable, honest.">"You'd have pulled me off. And then you'd know."</span>`,`She had nothing to say to that. Then he took his hands out of his pockets and asked her straight out, the way she'd taught him to ask everything. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Quiet, direct, afraid.">"What are you going to tell him?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Walk Fisk to Boyd's truck now and let him tell it with her there.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Nothing. You're going to tell him, and I'll be standing right there."</span> They walked down the row together. Fisk told it standing up straight, without dressing any of it, the six o'clock and the initials and the radio call, and Kit kept quiet till he was done. Boyd listened with his arm on the window. Then he looked at her. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Jet good?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Torqued and inspected."</span> Boyd got out, took the forms binder, and corrected the entry himself, the slow way the rules wanted, which took longer than the torque had. Then he turned to Fisk. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Firm, even, not unkind.">"Go home and sleep. Be here at six tomorrow. You're off torque tasks till Sergeant Darrow retrains you, and this goes up the line in writing. That part isn't mine to skip."</span> Fisk said yes, Master Sergeant, and went. At 8:55 two two eight rolled, twenty five minutes late, and Kit saluted it out.`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism, every piece in order. The jet stopped first. Fisk heard it from you first, in private. And Boyd heard it this morning, from the airman who did it, with you standing next to him. Not Monday, and not secondhand. On the test, covering for somebody lands low, and so does jumping past your supervisor. Once the jet was held, the hurry was over. The report still had to happen today.`},
        {t:`Wave Boyd up to the jet and tell him there, by the crew bus.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She waves him up. Boyd drives to the nose and gets out by the crew bus, where the aircraft commander and the boom operator are standing with their coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Sergeant Boyd, Fisk signed a torque he didn't do. It's fixed and inspected."</span> The boom operator stops drinking. Boyd looks at the aircrew, then at her. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"My truck. Both of you."</span> By the time they walk down the row, Fisk is the crew's story for the whole sortie.`,
          why:`The right report, to the right man, in front of an aircrew that didn't need it. Boyd needed it now. The crew didn't. Say a serious thing face to face, and let the person who decides who else hears it make that call.`},
        {t:`Take the forms past Boyd to the quality office in the hangar.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She walks the forms into the hangar to the quality office and lays them on the counter. The inspector there calls Boyd before she's back outside. Boyd meets her at the hangar door. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"I was forty yards away, Darrow."</span>`,
          why:`Going past your supervisor to the office above him, when he's forty yards off and waiting for you, is going over a head. Boyd sends it up the line. That's his job, and he does it. Your job was to bring it to him.`},
        {t:`Tell Boyd the wheel needed a recheck, and fix the entry herself.`, s:0, flag:`coveredforfisk`, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Left main needed a recheck, Sergeant. It's done and inspected."</span> Boyd nods. She corrects the entry herself at the truck, quietly, where nobody's looking. Fisk says thank you twice. She doesn't feel thanked.`,
          why:`You fixed the wheel and buried the record. The forms are clean and Boyd doesn't know why, and Fisk has learned that a false line goes away if the right person likes you. That's covering, and the test keys it near the bottom. Records get read. Someday somebody reads this one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s6`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Off nights`, q:`Boyd isn't asking about Fisk anymore. What should Kit say?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const cov=(s.flags||{}).coveredforfisk||(G||{}).coveredforfisk;return [`Boyd stayed put. He sat there until two two eight was off the ground and turning out over the river, then reached across and shoved the passenger door open. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Gruff, low, gentle.">"Get in. It's cold."</span> She got in. His heater still smelled like somebody had left a sock on it in 2016.`,`For a while he just watched the runway. Then, without looking at her, he said it. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, even, already decided.">"${cov?`Your jet went late on a wheel. `:``}You put Fisk on that wheel. He came in off ten hours at the warehouse."</span>`,`There was no anger in it. He said it like a thing he'd already decided, and was waiting on her to decide too.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him Kemp pulled his airman at six and left Fisk alone.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Kemp pulled his airman at six. Fisk was alone after that."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"I know what Kemp did."</span> He rolls the window up. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"I asked about you."</span>`,
          why:`True, and beside the point. He asked about your call, and you handed him someone else's. Pointing at a peer when your own part is the question lands low on the test, and lower in a truck.`},
        {t:`Tell him Fisk said he was good, and she took his word for it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"He said he was good. I took his word."</span> Boyd nods slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"He's twenty. He'll always say he's good."</span> He lets that sit. She knows he's right, and she knows she knew it at four.`,
          why:`Half of it. He did say it, and you did take it. You'd also seen the lanyard and the red ears, and it was four in the morning. You walked past it. Own the part you saw.`},
        {t:`Tell him yes, she saw it at four and let it go.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, owning it.">"I saw it at four, Sergeant, and again at the wheel. I let it go."</span> Boyd nodded at the windshield. Neither of them said anything for a good minute.`,
          why:`Integrity, the kind with no audience. He wasn't asking what happened. He was asking whether you'd own your share, and you did, in two short sentences, with no reason in front of them. The officer who owns the problem beats the one who explains it. Where do you suppose he learned to ask it that way?`},
        {t:`Tell him it's handled now, and she'd rather leave it there.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It's handled, Sergeant. I'd rather leave it there."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Sure."</span> He puts the truck in gear. The ride back to the shop takes three minutes and feels like twenty.`,
          why:`You shut a door your supervisor was holding open. Handled isn't an answer to what he asked. When the boss asks about your part, give him your part.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s7`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`My miss`, q:`How far short was the plan Vern briefed?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=s.picks||{},cov=(s.flags||{}).coveredforfisk||(G||{}).coveredforfisk;return [...(p[5]===undefined||p[5]===2?(cov?[`Then Boyd said it to the windshield. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Next time he's off nights, he's on the forms, not the wrench."</span> She said yes, Sergeant. He didn't ask anything else. If he had, she told herself, she'd have told him the rest, and she didn't believe that either.`]:[`Then Boyd said it to the windshield. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, gruff, approving.">"You said yours before I had to ask for it."</span> He put the truck in gear and sat there with his foot on the brake. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, gruff, almost tender.">"That's how I'd want the shop run when I'm gone."</span>`,`Her hand went to her chest pocket, to the work card from January with five weeks on the back of it. There was nothing she could say in that truck that wouldn't have been a lie to him or to herself.`]):[]),`At ten, with the jet out on the track and nothing to do but wait for it, she sat in the crew truck and finally took Vern's log out of her coat.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Navigator's log, April 2001" say="My log, twenty April, oh one. Tolliver to the track and home, six point one hours. Four receivers at the brief, fourteen thousand pounds each. Offload available, fifty thousand. The plan said four. My miss. Caught it at engine start. Told the aircraft commander before we taxied, and called the receivers' units. Gave number four what was left. Thirty minutes late. The aircraft commander said better here than on the track. I wrote it down so I'd remember whose it was." by="Vern">20 APR 01. TOLLIVER, TRACK, TOLLIVER. 6.1 HRS.<br>4 RECEIVERS AT BRIEF, 14,000 LB EACH. OFFLOAD AVAILABLE 50,000.<br>PLAN SAID 4. MY MISS. CAUGHT AT ENGINE START.<br>TOLD AC BEFORE TAXI. CALLED RECEIVER UNITS. GAVE NO. 4 WHAT WAS LEFT.<br>30 MIN LATE. AC: BETTER HERE THAN ON THE TRACK.<br>WROTE IT DOWN SO I'D REMEMBER WHOSE IT WAS.</prop>`,`She read it twice. In eight weeks of logs it was the first time she'd seen him get anything wrong, and he'd written it in ink, MY MISS, in the same capitals as everything else. He'd picked it on Tuesday for the arithmetic. He couldn't have known.`,`Hers was folded small under the foam in her toolbox, a score sheet a machine had printed in 2019. She'd never written a word on it. She'd told people whose it was instead, and every time she'd said Boyd.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Eight thousand pounds, what's left after three full loads.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Eight thousand is on the page, more or less. It's what number four got. It isn't how far short the plan was, and she reads the question again.`,
          why:`That's a real number from the problem, answering a different question. Three full loads leave eight thousand. The plan promised four, and four is fifty six thousand. How far short is what was promised, less what was there.`},
        {t:`Fourteen thousand pounds, one receiver's whole share.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fourteen thousand would mean number four got nothing. The log says he gave number four what was left, so something was left.`,
          why:`You took off a whole share when only part of one was missing. Do the multiplication before you decide what's short.`},
        {t:`Nothing at all; fifty thousand covers all four of them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She multiplies it again. Four times fourteen is fifty six. Fifty doesn't cover it, and Vern wouldn't have written MY MISS over nothing.`,
          why:`Four fourteens is fifty six thousand. Rough it first: fourteen is more than twelve and a half, and four twelve and a halfs is already fifty. The plan was over before you finished multiplying.`},
        {t:`Six thousand pounds, fifty six thousand against fifty.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Four times fourteen thousand is fifty six thousand. Fifty available. Six thousand short. She wrote it in the margin under MY MISS, small, in pencil, and sat looking at his ink for a while.`,
          why:`Multiply what was promised, then subtract what was there. Fifty six thousand less fifty thousand is six thousand. Eight thousand is what's left after three full loads, a different question with a real number in it. Which one did the question ask?`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s8`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`How was drill`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At two that afternoon, with the jet still on the track, she walked across the street to the base test office. The test control officer was a civilian with reading glasses on a chain and a drawer full of vouchers. It took ten minutes, a form and her ID, and she walked out with a voucher number printed on a slip of paper.`,`She'd sat the test in that building in October 2019, in the room at the end of the hall, and the clock in the hall was the same one. The test control officer tore the slip off, pushed it across, and mentioned that the office gave the computer version now too, end of the hall, any drill Saturday. Kit said Kansas City was fine. She folded the slip into her pocket with the work card and didn't look down the hall.`,`Graham was at the stove when she got home at six, with a pot of soup going and a stack of lab reports at his elbow. He looked at her face before he looked at anything else, and he turned the burner down before he asked. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"How was drill?"</span> It was the same thing he'd asked in January, at the same counter, and in January she'd answered with the want.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say it was long and the jet went late, and ask about his week.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Long. Jet went late. How were the sophomores?"</span> He tells her about the sophomores. He keeps looking at her while he does it, the way you'd watch a gauge that's reading a little off.`,
          why:`He asked a real question and you handed it back to him. That isn't privacy. It's a closed door. Keeping the people who count on you in the dark lands low on the test, at work or at home.`},
        {t:`Tell him the whole day, her own part in it included.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Fisk signed a torque he didn't do. I caught it."</span> Then she told him the rest, the crib and the hook and the truck and Boyd's window, and then the part she hadn't planned on telling: that she'd looked at Fisk at four in the morning and seen nine and a half hours on him and given him the wheel anyway. Graham stirred the soup. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Why didn't he just tell you he was wiped?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"He said I'd have pulled him. And then I'd know."</span> Graham wanted to know what she'd know. It took her a while. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, clear, realizing.">"That he couldn't."</span> She heard it come out of her own mouth and stood there at the counter with it. Graham waited, and after a while he got down two bowls.`,
          why:`Communication. He asked how drill was, and you told him what happened, your part included, without making yourself the hero of it. That's a straight report to the person it's owed to. The test wants the same at work: the whole picture, to the person who needs it, before they have to dig.`},
        {t:`Tell him about Fisk, and leave out who put Fisk on the wheel.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She tells him about Fisk and the crib and the hold, and it comes out a good story with her as the one who caught it. All of it's true. Graham says he's proud of her, and she lets him be, and the four o'clock part stays in her pocket with the work card.`,
          why:`Every fact true, and the one about you left out. That's a report shaped to make you look right. People who trim their own part out of an account get found out, and the test knows it. Whose part was missing?`},
        {t:`Tell him it was the kind of day that looks good to a colonel.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Good day for it, honestly. The kind that looks good to a colonel."</span> Graham laughs a little. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"That's what it was for?"</span> She says no, and starts to say what it was, and doesn't.`,
          why:`You turned a kid's worst morning into a line on your own record. That's the old habit, the wing's reasons standing in for yours. Is that really what the day was?`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s9`, domain:`Speed and Spatial`, task:`TR`, title:`Middle column`, q:`What does the scheduler show for the north center on May 15?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=s.picks||{},cov=(s.flags||{}).coveredforfisk||(G||{}).coveredforfisk;return [`${cov&&(p[7]===undefined||p[7]===1)?`She'd told Graham all of it but one thing, and it was the same thing she hadn't told Boyd. `:``}At ten that night she sat at the kitchen table with the laptop and the voucher slip, and booked it herself. Graham had gone to bed. He'd picked the date in February, and she'd let him, and tonight she wanted her own hands on it. It was the same chair she'd hit Cancel from in February. She didn't look for Cancel.`,`The scheduler gave her three Kansas City centers and three Saturdays in a grid, with the first open start time in each cell. Graham had said the north one, in the strip mall off the interstate. It was the closest to the river road.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Test scheduler, first open start time" say="Three centers down the side, three Saturdays across the top. The north center: May eighth, twelve thirty. May fifteenth, eight o'clock. May twenty second, nine o'clock. The south center: May eighth, eight o'clock. May fifteenth, twelve thirty. May twenty second, eight o'clock. The east center: May eighth, nine o'clock. May fifteenth, nine o'clock. May twenty second, none open.">CENTER · SAT MAY 8 · SAT MAY 15 · SAT MAY 22<br>KC North · 12:30 PM · 8:00 AM · 9:00 AM<br>KC South · 8:00 AM · 12:30 PM · 8:00 AM<br>KC East · 9:00 AM · 9:00 AM · none</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Read 12:30, the first column of the top row.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She reads 12:30 and nearly clicks it. That's May 8, one column to the left, a week too early and an afternoon start besides.`,
          why:`One column over. The dates run across the top, so May 15 is the middle column. Find the row, find the column, and only then read the cell.`},
        {t:`Read 9:00, the last column of the top row.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Nine o'clock is the north center, right row. It's May 22, one column too far, and a week past the date on the fridge.`,
          why:`Right row, wrong column. That's the slip this subtest is built to catch, and some of the wrong choices sit in the cells next door. Count the columns from the header, not from the edge.`},
        {t:`Read 8:00, the middle column of the top row.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Finger on the north row, finger on May 15. Eight o'clock. She checked next door both ways, 12:30 to the left and 9:00 to the right, and 12:30 below it in the south row, and clicked the eight. It took eleven minutes in all, most of it finding her ID number.`,
          why:`Row first, then column, then the neighbors, so you know what a slip would have looked like. Eight, with 12:30 and 9:00 on either side and 12:30 below. On the test, many of the wrong answers come from those cells.`},
        {t:`Read 12:30, second row, middle column.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Right column, one row down. That's the south center, and an afternoon start, five hours of test after a two hour drive.`,
          why:`One row down. Your finger slid off the north row on the way across. Keep one finger on the row and one on the column until they meet.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s10`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Sunday brief`, q:`Boyd gave her the brief, and Fisk is in the second row. How should Kit give it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const cov=(s.flags||{}).coveredforfisk||(G||{}).coveredforfisk,pc=(s.flags||{}).publiccorrection||(G||{}).publiccorrection;return [`The email came at 10:14.`,`<prop type="email" from="Pearson VUE" to="Katherine Darrow" time="Saturday, 10:14 PM" subject="Appointment confirmation">Exam: Air Force Officer Qualifying Test<br>Date: Saturday, May 15, 2027<br>Time: 8:00 AM<br>Location: test center, Kansas City, Missouri</prop>`,`On the way to bed she stopped at the fridge. MAY 15 was still in the corner of Graham's board, with its question mark. She licked her thumb and wiped the question mark off, and left the rest.`,`She was on the river road at 5:30. At 7:10 her phone buzzed in the shop.`,`<prop type="text" from="Graham" time="7:10 AM">Question mark's gone.<br>I'll drive.</prop>`,`Boyd ran Sunday roll call from the front of the shop. ${cov?`When it was over he caught her at the coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Your jet went late on a torque yesterday. You give the safety brief."</span>`:`Before it started he caught her at the coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"You give the safety brief. Yesterday. Your call how."</span>`} Fisk was in the second row, ${cov?`gray in the face, looking at his boots.`:pc?`rested for the first time in months by the look of him, and the airmen on both sides of him had left a little room.`:`rested for the first time in months by the look of him, looking at his boots.`}`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Make it about saying you're too tired and leave Fisk's name out.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She stood up front with her hands in her coat pockets. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Steady, carrying, plain.">"Yesterday my jet went twenty five minutes late on a wheel torque. Nobody got hurt. I put an airman on that wheel who came in off a night shift. It was all over him at four, and I gave him the wheel anyway. So here's how my crew runs now. If you're too tired to turn the wrench, you're too tired to sign for it, and you tell me. Nobody gets in trouble for telling me."</span> That was the whole brief. At the back Boyd had his arms folded and didn't unfold them, which was how he listened to things he agreed with. In the second row Fisk looked up from his boots.`,
          why:`Leadership and Communication. You told it as your own miss, which it was, and you kept his name out of it. He'd already answered for it to the people who had to hear it. Then you fixed what caused it. Nobody in your crew had a way to say I'm done in without paying for it. The test likes a leader who fixes the cause out loud and handles the person in private.`},
        {t:`Read the rule on signing for work out loud, and stop there.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She reads the rule on signing for work off her phone, word for word. It's correct. Half the shop is looking into its coffee by the second sentence, and the airman who most needed to hear something else hears nothing new.`,
          why:`Accurate and safe, and it skips the lesson the day taught. A rule read out loud tells people what they already know. Tell them what happened, without the name, and what changes now.`},
        {t:`Tell the whole story the way it happened, Fisk's name and all.`, s:0, flag:`publiccorrection`, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She tells it the way it happened, Fisk's initials and the six o'clock and the truck. Every head turns to the second row. Fisk keeps his eyes on his boots until she's done, and for a long while after.`,
          why:`He'd already answered for it to the people who had to hear it. Telling the whole shop, with his name in it, teaches nothing the story wouldn't teach without it. It only spends his dignity. Was that yours to spend?`},
        {t:`Hand the brief back to Boyd as his to give.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It's your flight, Sergeant. You should give it."</span> Boyd looks at her a while. Then he gives it, and it's fine, and it's his.`,
          why:`He handed you the brief on purpose. Giving it back ducks the one part of the weekend that was yours to say out loud. When a supervisor hands you a hard job in front of the shop, take it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s11`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Friday nights`, q:`Fisk says he could quit the warehouse. What should Kit say?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const cov=(s.flags||{}).coveredforfisk||(G||{}).coveredforfisk,pc=(s.flags||{}).publiccorrection||(G||{}).publiccorrection;return [`${pc?`Fisk kept clear of her all morning. Near noon he finally came over to her toolbox, and stood a step farther back than usual.`:`At noon Fisk sat down on the step of her toolbox cart with a gas station sandwich. Half of it was gone before he said a word.`} ${cov?`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"You didn't tell him. About the line."</span> She said she knew. `:``}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"I've been thinking. I could quit the warehouse. Then I'm not tired, and that's the whole problem fixed."</span> He said it like he'd made up his mind, and like he was hoping somebody would tell him he hadn't.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him the Guard comes first, and to give the warehouse notice.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Guard comes first. Give them notice."</span> Fisk nods like he's being told the weather. On Monday he gives notice. At April drill the civilian job on his sign-in sheet says tire shop, days, and she can guess what it pays.`,
          why:`You made a money decision for a twenty year old you don't pay. Tired was the problem, and you prescribed a cure for his whole life. Help him find his own fix.`},
        {t:`Tell him to ask the warehouse for Friday nights off before drill.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Ask them for Friday nights off before drill."</span> It's good advice. He says okay, and she can hear it's her plan now and not his, and that he'll ask in a way that makes it easy to say no.`,
          why:`Right answer, and it was yours, not his. A good fix still needs the person to own it. Ask the question that gets him there, and he'll fight for the answer.`},
        {t:`Tell him that's his business, as long as he shows up rested.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Your business. Just show up rested."</span> He nods. Next drill he shows up rested, mostly. The drill after that he comes in with red ears and the lanyard still on, and says he's good.`,
          why:`Fair, and it leaves him right where Saturday found him, tired and alone with it. He brought it to you. That's the moment to help.`},
        {t:`Ask what the overnights pay for, and plan drill Fridays with him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"What do the overnights pay for?"</span> He told her. His truck, and half his mother's rent since last spring. Quitting was the big answer, and he was twenty, and it was the only one he'd come up with. They worked it on the back of a work card. Drill was one weekend a month, so it was one Friday overnight a month, not the whole job. He decided he'd ask his lead for drill Fridays off, and what he'd say, and what he'd do if the answer was no. When he got up he stopped and looked at the jet, at the new wheel and the stencil under the window. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Quiet, hopeful, unsure.">"Am I still on her?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Warm, plain, certain.">"You're still on her."</span> He went back to the wheel well with the rest of his sandwich.`,
          why:`Mentoring. You asked the question that let him see how big the problem really was, one night a month, and then he fixed it himself. The test favors helping someone solve their own problem over solving it for them. Who's going to ask his lead on Monday, you or him?`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s12`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`VA`, title:`Pearson order`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Vern kept the heritage room on Saturdays, and on drill weekends he kept it Sundays too, when there were people on base to wander in. Nobody ever wandered in. Kit came at four, when the shop let go, with a pencil.`,`It was one long room in the old operations building: glass cases, a propeller blade on the wall, an attitude indicator off some long gone jet under glass, and the computer in the corner where he'd read the long rule. Vern had a table cleared and a stack of pages squared on it in his block capitals. His egg timer from home was sitting by the stack, the cracked one he'd glued.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Every block till now has been one subtest at my table. This is the order they'll run it in. Verbal Analogies first. Eight minutes."</span>`,`Before he'd turned the timer she had the order down the left margin of the first page, a subtest to a line with the minutes beside each one. She did it without thinking. Vern watched her do it. Then he turned the dial.`,`Item thirteen at four minutes, the way he'd taught her, and she was a little ahead. Item twenty two was the kind she liked. STANZA is to POEM as MOVEMENT is to blank.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write PROTEST in the blank.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Protest was the first thing her head handed her, the kind of movement people march in. But a stanza isn't a cause. It's a chunk of a poem, so she scratched protest out.`,
          why:`That's the movement people carry signs for. The first pair tells you which meaning the item wants. A stanza is a section of a poem, so a movement here is a section of a piece of music.`},
        {t:`Write SYMPHONY in the blank.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`A stanza is a section of a poem. A movement is a section of a symphony. She wrote it with forty seconds left and three items to go.`,
          why:`Part to whole. Movement here is the music word, one section of a long piece. Protest is the marching meaning, and tempo is something a movement has, not something it's part of. Did the first pair tell you which meaning to use?`},
        {t:`Write COMPOSER in the blank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Composer writes it, poet writes the stanza. That's some other sentence, not the one on his page.`,
          why:`You jumped to who makes it. The stem is part to whole, and a composer isn't the whole of anything.`},
        {t:`Write TEMPO in the blank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`A movement has a tempo. A stanza doesn't have a poem. It's part of one. The sentence runs the wrong way.`,
          why:`Right field, wrong relationship. A movement has a tempo, and it doesn't belong to one. Keep the direction: the part first, then the whole it belongs to.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f4s5`, title:`Now, or first?`, intro:`Kit had about a minute to decide whether the torque line could wait. Here are six more. Pick the move that comes first.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`An airman tells you they skipped a required check on a vehicle that leaves in ten minutes.`, choices:[`Talk with the airman privately next week`,`Stop the vehicle and tell your supervisor now`,`Do the check yourself and say nothing`], answer:1, why:`Something unsafe is still in motion. Stop it and report it now. The private talk still happens, after the vehicle's stopped.`},
      {prompt:`A peer keeps talking over you in staff meetings.`, choices:[`Talk with the peer privately`,`Tell your commander about it`,`Talk over them next time`], answer:0, why:`Nothing false or unsafe is moving. It's a working problem between two people, so it goes to the person first, in private.`},
      {prompt:`A cargo weight on a crew's load paperwork is one you know is wrong, and the aircraft takes off in thirty minutes.`, choices:[`Fix the number on the form yourself`,`Mention it to the crew after the flight`,`Tell the aircraft commander now`], answer:2, why:`A false number is headed for a jet. It's the exception: get it to the person who can stop it, now. Fixing the form yourself skips the people who have to know.`},
      {prompt:`Your airman's paperwork is sloppy, but it's accurate.`, choices:[`Go over it with them in private`,`Mark it up in front of the shop`,`Redo it yourself each time`], answer:0, why:`Nothing false, nothing unsafe. Coach the person privately. Redoing it yourself only teaches them it doesn't matter.`},
      {prompt:`You turned a tool back in to the crib this morning, and now you notice its socket is missing. Your jet starts engines in twenty minutes.`, choices:[`Look for it after the launch`,`Borrow a socket and say nothing`,`Tell the expediter and your supervisor now`], answer:2, why:`It's your own miss, and a loose socket near a running engine is unsafe and still in motion. Stop the start and report it yourself, now.`},
      {prompt:`A coworker complains to you about the new schedule.`, choices:[`Agree that it isn't fair`,`Listen, then tell them who can change it`,`Pass along what they said to your boss`], answer:1, why:`A complaint isn't a breach. Hear them out and point them to the person who can act on it. Carrying it upstairs for them goes behind their back.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Backing a government truck out of a tight spot, you scrape a concrete post and leave a small dent in the bumper. Nobody saw it happen. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Leave the truck where you parked it, and don't mention the dent.`,`Report the dent only if someone asks you about it later.`,`Pay to have it fixed yourself, then tell your supervisor.`,`Report it when you turn the truck in, and fill out the damage report.`,`Tell a peer what happened, and ask them what they would do about it.`], a:3, why:`Integrity and Professionalism. Your own mistake, reported by you, right away, through the normal process. Paying for it yourself is honest in spirit and skips the record. Asking a peer is stalling.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Backing a government truck out of a tight spot, you scrape a concrete post and leave a small dent in the bumper. Nobody saw it happen. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Leave the truck where you parked it, and don't mention the dent.`,`Report the dent only if someone asks you about it later.`,`Pay to have it fixed yourself, then tell your supervisor.`,`Report it when you turn the truck in, and fill out the damage report.`,`Tell a peer what happened, and ask them what they would do about it.`], a:0, why:`Saying nothing hides damage to government property and leaves someone else to find it and answer for it. Waiting to be asked is close behind, but at least it admits the dent if anyone asks.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your flight commander asks you to show your section's training completion as 90 percent on tomorrow's slide. The real number is 82 percent, and the last few airmen finish next week. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell your airmen to finish tonight, even if they have to rush it.`,`Talk to the flight commander privately, and offer 82 with the finish date.`,`Put 82 on the slide without saying anything to the flight commander first.`,`Ask the squadron's training manager whether 90 would be all right.`,`Put 90 on the slide as asked; the airmen will be done next week anyway.`], a:1, why:`Integrity with Communication. You won't report a false number, and you go to the person who asked, privately, with an honest number and what's behind it. Changing the slide without a word blindsides your boss. Rushing training to make a number true is its own problem.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your flight commander asks you to show your section's training completion as 90 percent on tomorrow's slide. The real number is 82 percent, and the last few airmen finish next week. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell your airmen to finish tonight, even if they have to rush it.`,`Talk to the flight commander privately, and offer 82 with the finish date.`,`Put 82 on the slide without saying anything to the flight commander first.`,`Ask the squadron's training manager whether 90 would be all right.`,`Put 90 on the slide as asked; the airmen will be done next week anyway.`], a:4, why:`Reporting a number you know is false is an integrity breach, whoever asked for it. That it'll be true next week doesn't make it true tomorrow.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. A box of replacement bolts for aircraft work arrives with a test certificate. It matches, word for word, a certificate you saw for a different lot last month, except for the date and the lot number. The bolts are on the shelf for Monday. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Pull the bolts and tell your supervisor and quality assurance today.`,`Email the supplier and ask them to explain the certificate.`,`Let the bolts go out on Monday; the supplier is on the approved list.`,`Set the bolts aside, and ask the supplier about it at the next delivery.`,`Pull the bolts quietly, and reorder from a different supplier.`], a:0, why:`This is the exception. A record that looks false is headed for aircraft, so you stop it and report it now. Emailing the supplier or waiting for the next delivery is safe but slow, and nobody who needs to know hears about it. A quiet reorder hides a problem other units may have too.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. A box of replacement bolts for aircraft work arrives with a test certificate. It matches, word for word, a certificate you saw for a different lot last month, except for the date and the lot number. The bolts are on the shelf for Monday. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Pull the bolts and tell your supervisor and quality assurance today.`,`Email the supplier and ask them to explain the certificate.`,`Let the bolts go out on Monday; the supplier is on the approved list.`,`Set the bolts aside, and ask the supplier about it at the next delivery.`,`Pull the bolts quietly, and reorder from a different supplier.`], a:2, why:`Letting parts with a doubtful certificate go onto aircraft is the worst outcome on the list. An approved supplier can still send a bad lot. That's why the paper exists.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your section has to pass a certification test on Friday. At the shared printer you pick up a printout that turns out to be the test's answer key, left by the test monitor. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Read it once, then put it back where you found it.`,`Hand it to your NCOIC, and ask them to deal with it for you.`,`Return it to the test monitor and tell them what happened.`,`Share it with your section so everyone has the same chance.`,`Shred it so nobody else can see it.`], a:2, why:`Integrity and Professionalism. Give it back to its owner and say plainly that you saw it, so they can decide whether the test needs changing. Handing it off makes it somebody else's problem, and shredding it hides that it was ever loose.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your section has to pass a certification test on Friday. At the shared printer you pick up a printout that turns out to be the test's answer key, left by the test monitor. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Read it once, then put it back where you found it.`,`Hand it to your NCOIC, and ask them to deal with it for you.`,`Return it to the test monitor and tell them what happened.`,`Share it with your section so everyone has the same chance.`,`Shred it so nobody else can see it.`], a:3, why:`Sharing it turns one accident into a whole section's cheating, and every certification on Friday becomes a false record. Reading it yourself is bad. Handing it to everyone is worse.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant at a squadron party. Another lieutenant, who's clearly had too much to drink, picks up their keys and says they're fine to drive home. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the bartender to stop serving them, and leave it at that.`,`Let them go, since they say they feel fine.`,`Tell them you'll call the commander if they get behind the wheel.`,`Text a few people in the squadron and ask someone to stop them.`,`Get them a ride or drive them yourself, and hang onto their keys.`], a:4, why:`Integrity and Leadership, and another case where you act now. Somebody's about to do something dangerous tonight, so you deal with it tonight, directly, and you make it easy for them to say yes. A threat or a group text is slower and louder than their keys in your pocket.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant at a squadron party. Another lieutenant, who's clearly had too much to drink, picks up their keys and says they're fine to drive home. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the bartender to stop serving them, and leave it at that.`,`Let them go, since they say they feel fine.`,`Tell them you'll call the commander if they get behind the wheel.`,`Text a few people in the squadron and ask someone to stop them.`,`Get them a ride or drive them yourself, and hang onto their keys.`], a:1, why:`Taking their word for it puts a drunk driver on the road. Feeling fine is what people say right before they shouldn't drive. Here, doing nothing is the worst thing on the list.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your commander changes the section's leave policy, and you think the change is a mistake. Your airmen ask you why it happened. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Explain the reason you were given, and object to the commander in private.`,`Tell them you'll look into it, then avoid the subject after that.`,`Tell them you don't know, and send them over to the first sergeant to ask.`,`Tell them you think it's a bad call and nobody asked for your opinion.`,`Email the commander your objections and copy your airmen on it.`], a:0, why:`Communication. Your airmen get the real reason from you, and your disagreement goes up the chain in private, where it can actually change something. Copying your airmen on an objection turns it into public pressure.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your commander changes the section's leave policy, and you think the change is a mistake. Your airmen ask you why it happened. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Explain the reason you were given, and object to the commander in private.`,`Tell them you'll look into it, then avoid the subject after that.`,`Tell them you don't know, and send them over to the first sergeant to ask.`,`Tell them you think it's a bad call and nobody asked for your opinion.`,`Email the commander your objections and copy your airmen on it.`], a:3, why:`Running down your commander's decision in front of your airmen undermines the commander and you both. It also changes nothing, because the person who could change it never heard it from you.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. You promised your airmen a training day off the line next week. This morning the squadron canceled it to cover an inspection. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Wait until the end of the week, then tell them.`,`Ask the squadron to reverse it before you tell your airmen anything at all.`,`Tell them yourself today, say why, and say when you'll try to reschedule.`,`Tell them it's canceled, and that the decision wasn't yours.`,`Tell them the squadron canceled it without a good reason.`], a:2, why:`Communication. Bad news goes early, from you, with the reason and what happens next. Pushing back on the squadron can still happen, but your airmen shouldn't be the last to know while you try.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. You promised your airmen a training day off the line next week. This morning the squadron canceled it to cover an inspection. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Wait until the end of the week, then tell them.`,`Ask the squadron to reverse it before you tell your airmen anything at all.`,`Tell them yourself today, say why, and say when you'll try to reschedule.`,`Tell them it's canceled, and that the decision wasn't yours.`,`Tell them the squadron canceled it without a good reason.`], a:4, why:`It isn't even true. There was a reason, and you know it. Blaming leadership with a false story damages trust in them and in you at once.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. An airman you supervise turns in a weekly report with several small errors for the third week in a row. It goes to the flight commander on Monday. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the flight commander the airman's reports can't be trusted.`,`Go over the errors with the airman, and set a check before it goes out.`,`Send it back with every error circled in red, and nothing else.`,`Fix the errors yourself each week and say nothing.`,`Ask another airman to check the reports before they get to you each week.`], a:1, why:`Mentoring and Communication. You go to the airman directly, find out where the errors come from, and set up a check that catches them. Circling everything in red marks the problem and teaches nothing.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. An airman you supervise turns in a weekly report with several small errors for the third week in a row. It goes to the flight commander on Monday. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the flight commander the airman's reports can't be trusted.`,`Go over the errors with the airman, and set a check before it goes out.`,`Send it back with every error circled in red, and nothing else.`,`Fix the errors yourself each week and say nothing.`,`Ask another airman to check the reports before they get to you each week.`], a:0, why:`Taking it to your boss before you've said a word to the airman damages their name and fixes nothing. Fixing it yourself is low too, but the report goes out right and nobody's hurt by it.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant on a joint exercise. A master sergeant from another squadron chews out one of your airmen in front of everyone for a mistake your airman really did make. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell your airman to go and apologize to the master sergeant again, in person.`,`Ask your own supervisor how to handle it before you do anything.`,`Tell the master sergeant, in front of everyone, to back off your airman.`,`Talk to your airman about the mistake, then the master sergeant in private.`,`Let it go, since it's the other squadron's business.`], a:3, why:`Communication and Leadership. The mistake was real, so your airman hears about it from you. How it was handled is between you and the master sergeant, in private. Both conversations happen at the lowest level.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant on a joint exercise. A master sergeant from another squadron chews out one of your airmen in front of everyone for a mistake your airman really did make. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell your airman to go and apologize to the master sergeant again, in person.`,`Ask your own supervisor how to handle it before you do anything.`,`Tell the master sergeant, in front of everyone, to back off your airman.`,`Talk to your airman about the mistake, then the master sergeant in private.`,`Let it go, since it's the other squadron's business.`], a:2, why:`You'd answer a public correction with a bigger public argument, with a senior NCO, in the middle of an exercise. Now two people are embarrassed in front of everyone, and the real mistake gets lost.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your day shift keeps leaving open problems for the night shift without passing them on. The night shift lead has complained to you twice. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the night shift lead to work it out with the day shift.`,`Post a memo to both shifts saying the day shift is at fault.`,`Start writing the handoff notes yourself at the end of each day.`,`Ask your commander to set a rule for shift handoffs.`,`Meet with both shift leads and agree on a handoff checklist.`], a:4, why:`Communication and Resource Management. You bring the two people who own the handoff together and leave with a fix they both built. Writing the notes yourself takes the job over, and the commander doesn't need to settle something you can.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Your day shift keeps leaving open problems for the night shift without passing them on. The night shift lead has complained to you twice. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the night shift lead to work it out with the day shift.`,`Post a memo to both shifts saying the day shift is at fault.`,`Start writing the handoff notes yourself at the end of each day.`,`Ask your commander to set a rule for shift handoffs.`,`Meet with both shift leads and agree on a handoff checklist.`], a:1, why:`Blaming one shift in writing, in front of both, guarantees the day shift digs in and the night shift gloats. It's a public correction that doesn't fix the handoff.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `With ten seconds left she put C on the last three, all of them, never slowed down, and the pencil was on the table before the timer rang.`,
    `Vern clicked the timer off and pulled the pages over. He left the answers alone. He went to the left margin of page one, the column she'd put down before he ever turned the dial.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Left margin, first page" by="Kit" say="Verbal Analogies, eight. Arithmetic Reasoning, twenty nine. Word Knowledge, five. Math Knowledge, twenty two. Reading Comprehension, thirty eight. Situational Judgment, thirty five.">VA 8<br>AR 29<br>WK 5<br>MK 22<br>RC 38<br>SJ 35</prop>`,
    `He put his finger on the fifth line. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Reading's twenty four. You wrote thirty eight."</span>`,
    `Thirty eight was the old paper number, from back before the test went on computers. Vern had never once said it at his table. She'd written it down anyway.`,
    `Kit kept quiet. Vern ran his eyes down the answer column to the bottom, C, C, C. Then he looked at her hands. Both of them were flat on the table with the pencil between, the way some man reading rules off a card had told a whole room to do it in 2019. Then he looked at her about as long as it takes to read one line to the period.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Quiet, certain, not asking.">"You've sat it before."</span>`,
    `He didn't wait for her yes. Egg timer to twenty nine, arithmetic pages over to her side of the table, and he sat back.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch04.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Rate Problems`
});
